11. International Labour Migration: Motives and Economic Effects
Paper: International Economics (ECNCC301) | Unit: II | Topic: Motives for labour migration, economic effects of labour movements
Introduction
International migration is the movement of labour across borders for work. It is one of the main forms of factor mobility, alongside capital flows, and has large effects on wages, output and development in both source and destination countries.
Motives for migration
• Wage differentials: the dominant economic motive. Workers move where real wages are higher.
• Employment opportunities: escaping unemployment and underemployment.
• Human capital returns: better education, training and career prospects (brain drain).
• Network effects: existing migrant communities reduce costs and risks.
• Non-economic factors: political instability, conflict, family reunion, climate and environment.
• Expected income (Harris-Todaro): migrants compare expected, not actual, incomes.
Effects on the receiving country
Using a simple supply-demand diagram, an inflow of labour lowers wages for competing native workers but raises the incomes of capital owners and complementary workers. Total national income rises (the immigration surplus), although the gain is usually small. Other effects: fills labour shortages, boosts entrepreneurship, supports ageing economies, but may strain public services and cause adjustment costs.
Effects on the sending country
Gains: remittances (a major source of foreign exchange and household income), skills and knowledge returning, relief of unemployment. Costs: brain drain of educated workers, loss of tax base and productivity, labour shortages in critical sectors such as health, and dependence on remittances. Brain gain via diaspora networks may offset losses.
Effects on world welfare
Migration from low-productivity to high-productivity regions raises world output. Some studies suggest that removing migration barriers would yield larger gains than removing trade barriers.
Trade and migration
In the H-O framework trade and migration are substitutes, since trade equalises factor prices and reduces the incentive to migrate. In models with technological differences they can be complements.
Conclusion
Migration generates net gains but uneven distribution. Policy should aim to maximise remittance benefits, reduce brain drain costs and protect migrants' rights.